| S236 | 中文翻译 — The Linux Kernel documentation（7.3.0 稳定版） — https://docs.kernel.org/translations/zh_CN/index.html | docs | B | The scope of the official Chinese kernel-documentation translation in the stable release, the effort's stated translation priority, and its own statement about where the manual pages live, for the lane's East-Asian coverage question | the 中文翻译 toctree; the 翻译计划 note under the tree's top-level heading; the 面向用户的文档 section and its TODOList; the closing sentence 也可参考独立于内核文档的 Linux 手册页 — the Linux Kernel documentation, blurb 7.3.0 | The Chinese tree in the stable release keeps the same breadth it had in the rolling build — the translation guide, the development-process set, the internal-API manuals, the development tools and test guide, the user-oriented documents, the firmware documents, the architecture documents, the uncategorised documents and the glossary — and still exposes no filesystems section; and it states the effort's own priority in the tree's opening note, soliciting translation contributions and naming the kernel users and administrators guide in particular, which is why the filesystem layer is unlikely to arrive there; and its user-oriented section closes by pointing readers at the manual pages, which are explicitly outside the kernel documentation, with the link to kernel.org/doc/man-pages | mined | 2026-10-23 | 180d | corpus/knowledge/kernel-doc-translation-scope-r28.md |
| S237 | Arch Linux 中文维基（Cloudflare CDN 镜像） — https://aw.lilydjwg.me/ | docs | B | The CDN-fronted, cached mirror of the Chinese ArchWiki that the wiki itself recommends when the primary host is unreachable, and the two offline documentation packages it names, for the lane's East-Asian reliability question | the served MediaWiki configuration (wgCurRevisionId 34322, wgArticleId 3495, wgPageName 首页, wgPageContentLanguage zh, MediaWiki generator 1.46.0); the site's local notice; the parser cache report line Cached time: 20260904125905 with Cache expiry: 864000 — the Cloudflare-fronted 首页 of the same wiki | The CDN host serves the **same** Chinese wiki deployment, not a distinct one: identical wiki name, identical MediaWiki generator 1.46.0, identical article id and revision id, and the same nine Chinese variant prefixes the primary host offers; what it adds is a **cached** render, with the parser cache report showing a cache time of 2026-09-04 and a 864000-second expiry, so a reader through the CDN may see a page state up to ten days behind the primary host; and the wiki's own local notice, served on it, names the two offline documentation packages arch-wiki-docs-zh-cn and arch-wiki-docs-zh-tw in the archlinuxcn repository and this CDN as the fallback when the site cannot be reached | mined | 2026-10-23 | 180d | corpus/knowledge/translation-provenance-r28.md |
| S238 | 繁體中文翻譯 — The Linux Kernel documentation（7.3.0 稳定版） — https://docs.kernel.org/translations/zh_TW/index.html | docs | B | The scope of the official Traditional-Chinese kernel-documentation translation, the effort's single named maintainer and its own outstanding sections, for the lane's East-Asian coverage question | the 繁體中文翻譯 toctree; the opening note 内核文檔繁體中文版的翻譯工作正在進行中; the per-section TODOList entries — the Linux Kernel documentation, blurb 7.3.0 | The Traditional-Chinese tree is the narrowest of the three East-Asian trees and the only one that names a single maintainer, 胡皓文, with a contact address; its coverage is the development-process set, the licensing rules, the development tools, the user and administrator guide and the reporting-issues page, and it exposes no filesystems section either, so the filesystem layer is outside all three East-Asian kernel-documentation trees; what it does carry is the longest explicit gap list of the three, nineteen entries spanning maintainer/index, core-api/index, driver-api/index, locking/index, subsystem-apis, doc-guide/index, the testing overview, kernel-hacking/index, rust/index, trace/index, fault-injection/index, livepatch/index, userspace-api/index, kbuild/index, tools/index, devicetree/index, firmware-guide/index, staging/index and the glossary, which is the effort's own statement of what remains untranslated; and it closes its user-oriented section by pointing readers at the manual pages, with the same kernel.org/doc/man-pages link the Simplified tree uses | mined | 2026-10-23 | 180d | corpus/knowledge/kernel-doc-translation-scope-r28.md |
| S239 | man intro (2): システムコールの説明 — https://ja.manpages.org/intro/2 | docs | B | The syscall-level error contract in Japanese, from the man-pages project's own Japanese translation, for the lane's errno-attribution frontier and its East-Asian coverage gap | §説明; §返り値; §この文書について; the 翻訳 language list — the Linux man-pages project, Japanese release 3.65 | The man-pages project maintains its own Japanese translation, and this page carries the syscall-level error contract that the kernel-documentation trees do not: on error most system calls return a negative error number, specifically the negation of one of the constants described in errno(3); the C library wrapper hides this from the caller, copying the absolute value into errno and returning -1 to the caller; the return value on success varies by system call, with most returning 0 but some returning a non-zero value; and the page points to syscalls(2) for the list of Linux system calls; the page's own language list is the decisive negative for the Chinese side, naming exactly seven translations, en, de, fr, ru, it, es and pl, and no Chinese one, so the man-pages project translates the syscall error contract into Japanese but not into Chinese | mined | 2026-10-23 | 180d | corpus/knowledge/errno-contract-eastasia-r28.md |
| S240 | errno(3) 日本語版 — Ubuntu manpages-ja — https://manpages.ubuntu.com/manpages/resolute/ja/man3/errno.3.html | docs | B | The complete errno symbol list with Japanese descriptions, for the lane's errno-attribution frontier and its East-Asian coverage gap | §エラー番号とエラー名; the full error-name list; §この文書について — the package manpages-ja-dev 0.5.0.0.20221215+dfsg-1build1, source manpages-ja, Linux man-pages release 5.10, Ubuntu resolute 26.04 LTS | The Japanese errno(3) carries the whole symbolic vocabulary with a Japanese description for each name, which is what a Japanese-language reader needs to tell the lane's failure classes apart: EACCES is 許可がない, EPERM is 操作が許可されていない, ENOSPC is デバイスに空き領域がない, EROFS is 読み出し専用のファイル・システムである, ESTALE is ファイルハンドルが古い状態になっている, EXDEV is 不適切なリンク, ETXTBSY is テキストファイルが使用中である, EBUSY is リソースが使用中である, EEXIST is ファイルが存在する, EOPNOTSUPP is ソケットでサポートしていない操作である, ENODEV is そのようなデバイスはない and EINVAL is 引数が無効である; it also states that errno is significant only when the call's return value indicated an error, that a successful call is permitted to change it, and that the numbers behind the names vary by UNIX system and by Linux architecture so the numeric values are deliberately not listed; the translation is a packaged Ubuntu localisation maintained across thirteen releases, from 14.04 LTS to 27.04 | mined | 2026-10-23 | 180d | corpus/knowledge/errno-contract-eastasia-r28.md |
| S241 | errno --- 标准 errno 系统符号（Python 3.13 简体中文文档） — https://docs.python.org/zh-cn/3.13/library/errno.html | docs | B | The errno symbol names with their Chinese descriptions, for the lane's errno-attribution frontier and the Chinese half of its East-Asian coverage gap | the module's intro paragraph; the errno.EPERM, errno.EACCES, errno.EBUSY, errno.EXDEV, errno.ENODEV, errno.ETXTBSY, errno.ENOSPC, errno.EROFS and errno.ESTALE entries — Python 3.13.17 documentation, zh-cn, translation source python-docs-zh-cn, page last updated 2026-10-22 | The Simplified-Chinese Python documentation carries the errno symbol names with Chinese descriptions, and it states the provenance the lane's attribution rule depends on: the names and descriptions are borrowed from linux/include/errno.h, which should be all-inclusive, and each symbol's value is the corresponding integer value; the descriptions the lane needs are EACCES 权限被拒绝 (mapped to PermissionError), EPERM 操作不允许 (also mapped to PermissionError), EBUSY 设备或资源忙, EXDEV 跨设备链接, ENODEV 无此设备, ETXTBSY 文本文件忙, ENOSPC 设备已无可用空间, EROFS 只读文件系统 and ESTALE 过期的 NFS 文件句柄; the mapping to PermissionError for both EACCES and EPERM is the Chinese-language statement that the two are one exception class with two different causes; the page also names the translation source, python-docs-zh-cn, so the rendering is versioned | mined | 2026-10-23 | 180d | corpus/knowledge/errno-contract-eastasia-r28.md |
| S242 | /boot writers for /boot/limine.conf — round 19 (C2) — corpus/knowledge/boot-writers-r19.md | own-work | A | The lane's own writer-attribution record for /boot/limine.conf, for the lane's hook-writer attribution and plymouth/snapper frontiers | § Writer classes (W-SNAPPER-CLEANUP, W-SNAPPER-PLUGIN, W-WATCHER); § Rejected readings; § Recheck — the lane's own entry, verified-at 2026-10-20 | The lane's own entry attributes /boot writes to named writer classes from three read-only captures, and each class is stated with a trigger, a mechanism, a signature and the writes it covers: W-SNAPPER-CLEANUP is driven by the snapper-cleanup.timer at OnBootSec=10m and OnUnitActiveSec=1h, and the local drop-in adds ExecStartPre=-touch and ExecStopPost=-rm -f plus an unguarded ExecStopPost=limine-snapper-sync, so the sync runs when the cleanup service stops, with a journal signature of _SYSTEMD_UNIT=snapper-cleanup.service and an Updated: /boot/limine.conf message and no Copied: or Saved: lines; W-SNAPPER-PLUGIN is the snapper post-action for subvolume / only, dispatching create-snapshot-post to run_sync add through a transient systemd-run unit, guarded by a skip when limine-snapper-sync.service is active or a watcher process matches, with a signature of a limine-snapper-add-<id>-<pid>.service unit and Copied:, Saved: and Updated: lines; and the entry states that the link between a snapshot creation and a sync is time coincidence plus the sudo record, not a process identity | mined | 2026-10-23 | 30d | corpus/knowledge/boot-writers-amend-2026-10-23.md |
| S243 | Btrfs space allocation and ENOSPC (round 30) — corpus/knowledge/btrfs-space-r30.md | own-work | A | The lane's own ENOSPC mechanism and decision table for the root btrfs filesystem, for the lane's errno-attribution frontier | § Scope; § How the space is split; § Decision table (D01-D10) — the lane's own entry, verified-at 2026-10-22 | The lane's own entry is the ENOSPC authority the lane already held, and it names the failure mode that a permission-shaped reading cannot see: on a host whose root is btrfs on LUKS with a single device, the data profile single and metadata and system DUP, a filesystem can return ENOSPC while df shows free space, because free space inside a data chunk cannot hold metadata, metadata reservations may overcommit only against unallocated space, and when there is no unallocated space to be had all reservations are kept within the free space in the allocated metadata chunks; the global reserve is the portion of metadata currently used for global block reserve, used for emergency purposes such as deletion on a full filesystem, and its size comes from the used bytes of the root, extent, checksum and free-space trees and is capped at 512 MiB, so it is reserved space and not free space; the entry's decision table turns the readings into operator actions, where a high metadata fill with unallocated space well above one metadata chunk is not yet an ENOSPC risk, while unallocated space near zero with metadata bytes_used over total_bytes near 100 percent means metadata reservations are confined to the existing chunks and the operator must free data chunks with a usage-filtered balance, and snapshots keep shared extents allocated so deleting files in the original frees nothing they still reference | mined | 2026-10-23 | on-release | corpus/knowledge/btrfs-space-repair-amend-2026-10-23.md |
| S244 | Btrfs repair and maintenance decisions (round 31) — corpus/knowledge/btrfs-repair-r31.md | own-work | A | The lane's own repair and maintenance decision table for btrfs, for the lane's btrfs/overlayfs frontier and its system-repair scope | § Decision table (Q01-Q08); § Error patterns — the lane's own entry, verified-at 2026-10-22 | The lane's own entry turns eight btrfs situations into named operator decisions with their source lines: defragmentation does not preserve extent sharing and may increase data-space consumption; a mount failure whose backtrace resembles the documented sample is cleared with btrfs rescue zero-log, which may lose changes since the last transaction commit, up to 30 seconds by default or less; a structural check should be preceded by unmounting and the default check does not modify the device, and --repair should not be used without developer or experienced-user advice and acceptance that not all corruption can be repaired; autodefrag is default off and detects small random writes in the tens-of-kilobytes range, currently 64 KiB; the commit interval defaults to 30 seconds with a warning above 300; nodatacow implies nodatasum and disables compression; device replacement requires the target to be the same size as or larger than the source and the filesystem to be resized; and discard=sync trims immediately but is not recommended because of possible severe performance impact, while discard=async batches freed extents and throttles I/O, with periodic fstrim suitable as a periodic service and no universal winner between the two | mined | 2026-10-23 | on-release | corpus/knowledge/btrfs-space-repair-amend-2026-10-23.md |
| S245 | mkinitcpio（源文） — Arch Linux 中文维基 — https://wiki.archlinuxcn.org/wzh/index.php?title=Mkinitcpio&action=raw | docs | B | The Chinese-language rendering of the **mkinitcpio hook API itself** — the four runtime hook functions, the post-hook argument contract, the BusyBox-versus-systemd rule and the recovery-shell parameter — plus the page's own per-page translation provenance, for the lane's mkinitcpio initramfs-hook frontier | the `翻译状态` template invocation at the head of the wikitext, `{{翻译状态\|mkinitcpio\|2025-12-27\|858491}}`; §钩子（HOOKS）→ 构建时钩子 / 运行时钩子 / 映像后处理钩子; §常用钩子 table, `base` row; §疑难解答 → 无法访问控制台，root 用户被锁定 — upstream revision 858491, translation date 2025-12-27 | The raw wikitext states the four runtime hook functions with their Chinese semantics: `run_earlyhook` runs once the API filesystems are mounted and the kernel command line has been parsed; `run_hook` runs shortly after the early hooks; `run_latehook` runs after the root device is mounted and should be used cautiously for further root-device setup or for mounting other filesystems such as `/usr`; `run_cleanuphook` runs as late as possible and in the **reverse** of the order in which the HOOKS array lists them, for final cleanup such as shutting down daemons started by the early hooks; and the page carries the decisive rule that runtime hooks are used only by the BusyBox init, because the `systemd` hook triggers a systemd-based init that runs no runtime hooks at all and uses systemd units instead; it also states the post-hook argument contract — the kernel used (possibly empty), the initramfs image path, and the optional unified kernel image path — with the `KERNELVERSION` and `KERNELDESTINATION` environment variables set; that a systemd-based initramfs is the default from mkinitcpio version 40; and that the `base` hook is optional under systemd and provides only a BusyBox recovery shell, reached by temporarily adding `SYSTEMD_SULOGIN_FORCE=1` to the kernel parameters | mined | 2026-10-23 | 180d | corpus/knowledge/mkinitcpio-hook-api-zh-r29.md |
| S246 | PKGBUILD（源文） — Arch Linux 中文维基 — https://wiki.archlinuxcn.org/wzh/index.php?title=PKGBUILD&action=raw | docs | B | The Chinese-language rendering of the **backup and drift rules** — the `.pacnew` and `.pacsave` semantics — plus the page's own per-page translation provenance, for the lane's Arch packaging-and-hook-semantics frontier and its `.pacnew`/`.pacsave` owned surface | the `翻译状态` template invocation at the head of the wikitext, `{{翻译状态\|PKGBUILD\|2025-03-22\|827943}}`; §其它 → backup; §install; §完整性检验 — upstream revision 827943, translation date 2025-03-22 | The raw wikitext states the drift rules the lane's owned surface depends on: on upgrade the new version is written as `''file''.pacnew` so that a file the user has modified is not overwritten; on removal the file the user modified is kept as `''file''.pacsave` unless the package is removed with `pacman -Rn`; the `backup` array must list **relative** paths, not paths beginning with a slash; and it states that the `backup` array supports neither empty directories nor `*`-style wildcards; the same page gives the scriptlet table with the new-then-old argument order for the upgrade pair, the rule that every function runs chrooted into pacman's installation directory, the rule that the script must not end with `exit`, and the checksum strength order `b2` > `sha512` > `sha384` > `sha256` > `sha224` > `sha1` > `md5` > `ck` | mined | 2026-10-23 | 180d | corpus/knowledge/pkgbuild-drift-rules-zh-r29.md |
