| S269 | Journal (jbd2) — the ext4 filesystem's journal documentation — https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/filesystems/ext4/journal.html | docs | A | What ext4's own journal guarantees after a crash, as the upstream counterpart to the btrfs checksum model the lane already holds in `corpus/knowledge/storage-health-r26.md` | the `3.6. Journal (jbd2)` section, the `data=ordered` guarantee paragraph — the `7.3.0` build banner in the page footer | ext4 by default writes **only filesystem metadata** through the journal, so file *data* blocks are **not** guaranteed to be in any consistent state after a crash; `data=journal` writes all data through the journal and is slower but safest, while `data=writeback` does not flush dirty data before the metadata — so the guarantee is a *mount-option-dependent* ordering property, not a property of the filesystem, and it is a different class of claim from btrfs's checksum-plus-scrub detection | mined | 2026-10-24 | on-release — compare the pinned `7.3.0` build banner against the current kernel release list; the paragraph is reworded between releases, so re-read before reusing the guarantee | corpus/knowledge/ext4-journal-guarantee-r32.md |
| S270 | Journal Checkpoint — the ext4 journal's checkpoint ioctl — https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/filesystems/ext4/journal.html | docs | A | The userspace-triggered journal checkpoint that the ext4 documentation itself ties to snapshotting — the mechanism the lane's snapshot and rollback surface would have to name to be credible | the `Journal Checkpoint` section of `3.6. Journal (jbd2)`, the `EXT4_IOC_CHECKPOINT` ioctl — the `7.3.0` build banner | the `EXT4_IOC_CHECKPOINT` ioctl forces a journal checkpoint from userspace, and its `EXT4_IOC_CHECKPOINT_FLAG_DISCARD` and `EXT4_IOC_CHECKPOINT_FLAG_ZEROOUT` flags cause the journal blocks to be discarded or zero-filled after the checkpoint completes, with the two flags mutually exclusive — the upstream documentation states outright that the ioctl "may be useful when snapshotting a system or for complying with content deletion SLOs", which is the ext4-side half of the lane's snapshot question, and it also names a content-deletion obligation no snapshot story in the lane has yet addressed | mined | 2026-10-24 | on-release — compare the pinned `7.3.0` build banner against the current kernel release list; re-read the flag semantics before reusing them | corpus/knowledge/ext4-journal-guarantee-r32.md |
| S271 | The Contents of inode.i_block — the ext4 filesystem's inode block-mapping structures — https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/filesystems/ext4/ifork.html | docs | A | How ext4 maps a file's logical blocks to disk, as the counterpart to the lane's btrfs block-group and space-accounting surface in `corpus/knowledge/btrfs-space-r30.md` | the `4.2. The Contents of inode.i_block` section, the extent-tree paragraphs and the extent-header table — the `7.3.0` build banner | ext4 replaced the classic `i_block` block map with an **extent tree**: the file's data blocks are reached through a tree of extent nodes whose root is stored in `inode.i_block`, which "allows for the first four extents to be recorded without the use of extra metadata blocks"; the tree is **at most 5 levels deep**, and the classic indirect map's 2\*\*32-block ceiling is the limit the tree exists to escape | mined | 2026-10-24 | on-release — compare the pinned `7.3.0` build banner against the current kernel release list; the tree depth and header layout are stable but the prose is not | corpus/knowledge/ext4-block-map-r32.md |
| S272 | Block Maps — the ext4 filesystem's classic block-map table — https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/filesystems/ext4/blockmap.html | docs | A | The classic ext4 block map itself, kept as a separate locator because the upstream documentation still serves it as a standalone page even though it no longer appears in the ext4 table of contents | the block-map table, its `Direct map to file blocks 0 to 11.` and `Indirect block:` rows — the page's own `<no title>` title element | the classic ext4 block map is a fixed 15-slot table in `i_block`: slots 0-11 are a direct map to file blocks 0-11, slot 12 points to an indirect block, and the remaining slots to double- and triple-indirect blocks; the page is still served at `latest` but carries **no heading and no table-of-contents entry** (`<no title>`), so a reader who arrives from the ext4 index never reaches it — the extent tree in `ifork.html` is what replaced it | mined | 2026-10-24 | on-release — compare the pinned `7.3.0` build banner against the current kernel release list; the block-map table itself is stable but its presence in the built documentation is not | corpus/knowledge/ext4-block-map-r32.md |
