| S277 | intro(2) — introduction to system calls — the upstream man-pages page for the syscall error contract — https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/intro.2.html | docs | A | The upstream statement of the syscall error contract itself, which is the row any behavioural claim about `errno` must cite rather than a translation | the `RETURN VALUE` section of `intro(2)` — the man-pages 6.19 footer line | on error most system calls return a negative error number, and it is the **C library wrapper** that hides the detail: the wrapper copies the absolute value into `errno` and returns -1 to the caller — so "the syscall returned -1" is a statement about the wrapper's convention, not about the kernel's return value, and the `errno` value the caller sees is the wrapper's copy of the kernel's negative number | mined | 2026-10-24 | on-release — compare the man-pages version in the footer against the current man-pages release; the contract wording is stable across releases but the footer version is the pin | corpus/knowledge/zh-syscall-error-contract-r32.md |
